<figure id="S8.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2604.04834v1/e2vid_vis.png" id="S8.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/191;" width="476" height="191" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S8.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S6</span>: </span><span id="S8.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Reconstruction results of different E2VID implementations under <math id="S8.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> lux. Each row shows a continuous temporal sequence (<math id="S8.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{t}_{1}\sim\text{t}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>t</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mtext>t</mtext><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{t}_{1}\sim\text{t}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>) corresponding to six consecutive policy inference steps.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S8.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Continuous recurrent reconstruction produces low-contrast and blurry images due to the reduced event rate caused by slow system operation.
On-demand recurrent variant generates more distinct images, but preserving the recurrent state introduces duplication artifacts between successive inferences, which confuses the policy. Removing the recurrent connection eliminates this artifact and yields the clearest reconstructions among the three variants. However, the absence of temporal accumulation, together with the unstable event rate during manipulation, leads to discontinuities and intermittent signal loss.
As a result, the three implementations achieve only <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\sim 50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\sim 50\%</annotation></semantics></math> success rates on average.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These observations suggest that reconstruction-based pipelines introduce additional computational overhead and implicitly assume temporally stable event streams. However, in VLA manipulation, event generation is tightly coupled with robot actions and inference frequency, resulting in irregular event dynamics that reduce the reliability of reconstruction-based event perception.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Efficiency Analysis</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the computational overhead, we report the additional parameters and FLOPs introduced by each method compared to the image baseline.
As shown in Table. <a href="#S9.T8" title="Table S8 ‣ 9 Efficiency Analysis ‣ E-VLA: Event-augmented Vision-Language-Action Model forDark and Blurred Scenes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S8</span></a>, the proposed methods (Overlay and Adapter) introduce only a small computational overhead compared to enhancement and reconstruction methods.
The simple overlay strategy directly fuses events with RGB inputs and therefore introduces no additional parameters and negligible computation.
Our learnable adapter module is also lightweight, adding only <math id="S9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13.3M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>13.3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13.3M</annotation></semantics></math> parameters and <math id="S9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20.4G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20.4G</annotation></semantics></math> FLOPs, which is substantially lower than several existing event-based approaches such as EvLight and E2VID.
These results demonstrate that our methods achieve effective event utilization while maintaining favorable computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S9.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S9.T8.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table S8</span>: </span><span id="S9.T8.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Additional parameters and FLOPs compared to the image baseline. E2VID is measured on a single inference with 2000 events, while other methods are measured with a <math id="S9.T8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="260\times 346" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>260</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>346</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">260\times 346</annotation></semantics></math> input resolution. Lower is better. Abbreviations: R-Net (RetinexNet), R-former (Retinexformer).</span></figcaption>
